Recursion
What is Recursion?
A function that calls itself is called a recursive function. The process is called recursion.

Factorial Example
cint fun(int n) {
    int a;
    if(n <= 0) return 1;
    a = n * fun(n-1);
    return a;
}

void main() {
    int a;
    a = fun(4);
    printf("%d", a);
}
// O/P: 24
Recursion trace for fun(4):
fun(4)
  └─→ 4 * fun(3)
          └─→ 3 * fun(2)
                  └─→ 2 * fun(1)
                          └─→ 1 * fun(0) = 1
                      = 2 * 1 = 2
              = 3 * 2 = 6
      = 4 * 6 = 24

Factorial Alternative
cint fun(int n) {
    if(n <= 0) return 1;
    return n / fun(n-1);  // Note: int division
}

void main() {
    printf("%d", fun(4));
}
// 4/1 = 3/2 = 2/1 = 1/1 = 4 (approx)

Recursive with Printing
cvoid test(int L) {
    printf("%d", L);
    if(L == 2)
        test(L+1);
    printf("%d", L);
}

void main() {
    test(1);
}
// O/P: 1  2  3  2  1  (prints going in and coming back)

Key Rules for Recursion

Every recursive function must have a base case (termination condition) — if(n <= 0) return 1;
Without base case → infinite recursion → stack overflow.
Order of execution in nested calls: inner to outer.


